<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Entitlements for the BUNDLED DOWNLOADER TOOLS ONLY — never for the app
	  itself. That is ffmpeg and ffprobe in Contents/MacOS/, plus every Mach-O
	  in the yt-dlp one-directory tree at Contents/Resources/yt-dlp/ (the
	  `yt-dlp_macos` launcher and the 106 dylib/so files under `_internal/`).
	  scripts/codesign-app.sh phase 0 applies this file; macampMac.entitlements
	  stays the app's own, and nothing here leaks into it.

	  NO DOUBLE HYPHEN MAY APPEAR IN THIS COMMENT. XML forbids it, and codesign
	  parses entitlements with AMFI's strict parser, not with plutil's lenient
	  one: `plutil -lint` reports OK on a file containing one while
	  `codesign` refuses it outright with

	      Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 19

	  which fails EVERY tool signature in phase 0. This file shipped in exactly
	  that state (the prose spelled the codesign flag with its leading hyphens)
	  and the defect survived because ad-hoc releases never call
	  codesign-app.sh at all. Spell flags without their hyphens here.

	  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation is LOAD-BEARING, not
	  defensive. `yt-dlp_macos` is a PyInstaller build that dlopen()s a CPython
	  runtime and a pile of `.so` extension modules at run time. In the
	  one-directory shape those files live beside the launcher in `_internal/`
	  and phase 0 re-signs every one of them with our own identity, so library
	  validation would in principle be satisfied. The entitlement is kept
	  anyway, for two reasons that are cheaper than the failure they prevent:

	    * enumeration is a claim, not a guarantee. A single Mach-O phase 0
	      failed to reach keeps its upstream ad-hoc signature, and library
	      validation refuses any library not signed by the same Team ID as the
	      main executable (or by Apple). The result is a code-signature kill at
	      the first dlopen, whose only user-visible symptom is that every
	      download fails.
	    * it is a RELEASE-ONLY failure. Dev and CI builds are ad-hoc signed with
	      no hardened runtime, so library validation is never armed and the tool
	      runs fine — which is exactly why this has to be written down rather
	      than found once Developer-ID signing is switched on.

	  Scope: this weakens the loader policy for these helper processes and for
	  nothing else. They are separate short-lived processes launched by the app,
	  not code loaded INTO it, so the app's own hardened runtime is untouched.
	  Apple's notarization service accepts this entitlement without a
	  provisioning profile; no other exception (JIT, unsigned memory, DYLD
	  variables) is requested, and none should be added without the same kind of
	  measured reason.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
